get is not the only way to create an object. You can also use the new operator. When you do this, you create an object and set aside enough memory for it on the heap before the object is returned:

Object obj = new Object();

This is known as instantiation. The object is now located in the heap. As with get, a reference to the object is returned, which you can assign to a variable.

Instantiation has the advantage that you can set initial values for object attributes:

Object obj = new Object(5);

obj.value = obj.value + 1;

System.out.println(obj.value); // prints: 6

In this case, an object is created and its attribute value is set to 5. This value is then increased by  as per the instruction obj.value = obj.value + 1;.

